 { The first important type of outpost to make note of in your own games is one that we have run into already in some of our previous examples, but that we have not paid any special attention to. I have not heard any author use a specific term to describe such an outpost, so we'll be referring to them as 'power outposts'. A power outpost is an outpost that is controlled by two pawns instead of one. Such outposts turn out to be extremely strong and valuable, as the outposted piece cannot be easily dislodged. Let's begin by looking at a position where Black has a 'normal' outpost on d4. } 1. { -KEY- } Nxd4 { White starts off by removing the outposted piece. } Bxd4+ { Black immediately replaces the outposted piece with another one. } 2. Be3 $1 ( { However, this time Black has no choice but to abandon the outpost, either by exchanging pieces with } 2. Be3 Bxe3 { or by retreating the bishop. } ) 
Bg7 ( { Black cannot play a move like } 2... Kg7 $2 { since after } 3. Bxd4 cxd4 4. Ne2 $18 { the isolated pawn on d4 will be lost. } ) 
3. Ne2 $16 { The outpost has not proven problematic for White, and in fact they stand better due to their greater space control. Next, White will likely play a2-a4-a5 with good chances to play for the win. In this example, we saw a regular outpost on d4. However, let's now consider a similar outpost on d4, but in this case Black is defending it with two pawns instead of one. } 
