 { Here, the Black knight is protected by two pawns instead of one. White cannot force the knight to leave, making such a piece very difficult to combat. Peter Romanovsky introduced the term 'permanent piece' to describe invaders of this kind, in his book 'Soviet Middlegame Technique'. } 1. { -KEY- } Bxd4 { Of course, the knight can be exchanged. However, this will change the structure and automatically leave Black with a protected passed pawn that will, in many cases, be just as strong of an asset as the outposted piece that it is replacing. } ( 1. b4 { Most often the best way for the defending side to fight a power outpost is by trying to target one, or both, of the supporting pawns. This kind of 'flank strategy' tends to be a better choice than capturing the outposted piece, as in this way the defending side can hope to avoid leaving their opponent with a protected passed pawn. We'll see a couple of examples of this technique later. } ) 
exd4 $11 { The position remains around equal, but in practical terms such positions can be challenging to play due to the long-term danger posed by Black's passed pawn. While exchanging the outposted piece usually leads to a protected passed pawn, that is not always the case. However, it typically leads to the formation of some type of asset. Let's illustrate what we mean by this in the next example. } 
