 { Here because White has a pawn on d3, the move 1.Bxd4 will not leave Black with a protected passed pawn. However, the exchange on d4 is still not desirable as Black will gain other assets. } 1. { -KEY- } Bxd4 $6 { Black has a pleasant choice of which way to take, with both recaptures being approximately equal in value. } exd4 { Black opens up the e-file and secures a powerful new advanced outpost on e3. They are also threatening to jump with their knight to e5, after which the d3 pawn will be impossible to defend. } ( 1... cxd4 { This approach may be even stronger than our text move. Here Black wins the powerful c5 square for their knight. } 2. Ke2 Nc5 { Given another move, Black will anchor their knight on c5 by playing ...a5, controlling the b4 square. So White should act before it's too late. } 3. b4 Na4 $1 { However, this opens up a new route towards the c3 square, the other outpost that was created after 1.Bxd4. } 4. Kf2 { Sidestepping the check on c3. } Nc3 $17 { Black has a dominant position on the queenside, with a pawn break like ...a5 likely to come next, looking to open up lines. White faces an unpleasant defensive task ahead. } ) 
2. Rbd1 $2 { Let's play a logical but poor move, to help us picture just how easy it is for White's position to collapse. } ( 2. Rfd1 $1 { This was the only way to keep the game alive. The idea is to meet 2...Ne5 with 3.Nf1! when White's knight from f1 is able to play a key role in safeguarding some of White's vulnerable dark squares on e3 and g3. } Ne5 3. Nf1 $1 f4 $15 { Black has an edge with more space and better placed pieces, but White keeps good chances to hold the balance after } 4. b4 $1 { , generating play on the queenside. } ) 
Ne5 $1 3. Nb1 Re7 { While the e5 square where the knight is sitting is technically not an outpost, it functions exactly as one because White can never play f3-f4 without leaving themselves with an outpost on g4. Incidentally, this is an example of a 'pseudo outpost', the topic covered in the final section of this chapter. } 4. Rfe1 Rae8 $1 { Threatening ...Nxd3+, when the White rook on d1 would be overloaded. } 5. Kf1 g5 $1 { Black finds the right moment to break through, when White's pieces are extremely passive. } 6. hxg5 Ng6 { The threat is to exchange rooks and then target both d3 and g2, the base of White's central and kingside pawn chains. } 7. Rxe7+ Rxe7 { White is completely lost. For example: } 8. g3 Re3 9. Kf2 Ne5 $19 { Black's infiltration is decisive, and White can resign. As you can see, power outposts can lead to a lot of problems for the defending side. Let's take a look at a series of typical situations next. } 
