 { White stands better here, with the queenside action favoring them as they have weak squares on b5 and c4 that they can target. However, things are about to immediately sour for them with their next move. } 1. { -KEY- } c4 $2 { A serious positional mistake. White leaves a power outpost on b4 and at the same time kills off the possibility of attacking the weak squares with moves like Bb5 or the knight maneuver Nd2-c4. } ( 1. Bb5 $1 { It was important to first occupy the outpost. } Ba6 2. c4 $16 { In this case, advancing the pawn would be strong. Both sides are left with a power outpost (b5 for White, b4 for Black), but the difference is that White is already occupying the square while none of the Black pieces can reach b4. } ) 
Ba6 $1 { Perhaps White thought that the b5 power outpost that they gained with the move 1.c4 made up for the weakness on b4. However, the problem is that Black is well-placed to cover the square, while the same does not hold true for White. } 2. Nd2 ( { The plan to maneuver to the power outpost with } 2. Ne2 $6 { fails tactically after } Rb4 $1 3. Nc3 $2 Bxc4 $17 { snatching a pawn. } ) 
 ( 2. Bd2 { Covering the outpost may be best, but here Black can uncork a nice sacrificial idea. } Rb4 $1 { A clever positional exchange sacrifice (more on this topic later in the course). Black invades the White position and prepares to double up rooks along the b-file. As for White, they can only try to passively hold things together, since capturing the rook is not a good idea: } 3. Bxb4 $2 cxb4 $17 { Securing a protected passed pawn, a strong outpost on c5, and long-term pressure against White's backward c4 pawn. The absence of open files leaves the rooks passive and Black in control. } ) 
 ( 2. Rb5 { Unfortunately for White, their own exchange sacrifice is not as convincing. } Bxb5 3. cxb5 Nb6 $17 { Black's pieces are well-placed, with the passed pawn safely blockaded as well as pressure against the a4 pawn and cover of the important c4 square. Black stands slightly better and can hope to break in the centre in the long run. } ) 
Rb4 $1 { Black is first to enter their opponent's half of the board. } 3. f4 { In the game, White tried to mobilize their central pawn majority since they understood that a fight on the queenside had become a losing proposition. } Rab8 $15 { Doubling up rooks. Objectively, the position remains close to equal but psychologically the avoidable loss of control over the queenside made White's position extremely challenging to manage. White blundered a couple of moves later and lost the game. While a lot of this chapter has revolved around landing minor pieces on outposts, we can see that power outposts are very helpful for rooks. This is because they can lead to attractive exchange sacrifices. Let's take a look at an example featuring rooks next. } 
