 { White has a superior minor piece, but Black has the better structure, making the position close to balanced. However, there is one move White can play that will make Black's defense very difficult. Can you find it? } 1. { -KEY- } Rc4 $1 { Correct! White identifies a power outpost on c4 and plants the rook there immediately! Suddenly the b4 pawn is in danger, while White can also look to gain the upper hand on the c-file with the move Rhc1 in the future. } a5 { Defending the pawn. } 2. Kb2 $1 { Preparing to play the other rook to a1. } ( { The problem with playing } 2. Rhc1 $6 { is that after } Rxc4 3. Rxc4 Rxc4 4. dxc4 $11 { the endgame is drawn due to the limited material on the board. } ) 
Rxc4 { Black is compelled to capture the rook, improving White's pawn structure. The reason is that otherwise Black could not shift either rook to the defense of the a5 pawn. } 3. dxc4 { Besides Ra1, White also has threats of expansion on the kingside that must be dealt with. } f6 { Stopping g4-g5. } ( 3... a4 $2 { This is one very natural way that Black can go wrong. Exchanging the vulnerable a5 pawn for White's healthy c4 pawn is a tempting proposition, but Black should not forget the power of outside passed pawns in the endgame! } 4. bxa4 Rxc4 5. Re1 { Defending the knight. } Kf8 6. a5 $1 { Black is not in time to reach the pawn with their king. } Ke8 7. Kb3 Rc8 8. a6 { White is winning. For example: } Ra8 9. Ra1 Kd7 10. Kxb4 Kc7 11. Rc1 { The simplest, the rook will position itself on the c6 outpost next. } Kd7 12. Rc6 $18 ) 
4. Ra1 Ra8 5. Kc2 $1 { White seeks to improve their king. } Kf7 6. Kd3 Ke8 7. c5 $1 { A temporary pawn sacrifice in order to break into the Black position. } dxc5 8. Kc4 $16 { Black faces a very unpleasant defensive task ahead, with survival being uncertain as White has dominant pieces everywhere on the board. It seems that the path is already very narrow, with the engine suggesting only ...h6 or ...Bd8 as worth trying. The more intuitive path of ...Kd7 fails to a nice tactical point: } Kd7 $2 { Now White has a tactical shot! } 9. d6 $1 Bd8 ( 9... Bxd6 $2 { loses immediately after } 10. Rd1 { when there is no way to save the piece, since ...Ra6?? fails to Nxc5+ } ) 
10. Kd5 $1 { From here Black may still be able to hold with computer-like precision, but from a human point of view the position is lost. The rest of the game is included with limited commentary. } Bb6 11. h4 a4 { Trying for counterplay. } 12. g5 fxg5 $2 { Giving White a second passed pawn. } 13. hxg5 a3 { The protected passed pawn is strong, but not enough. } 14. f6 $1 gxf6 15. gxf6 $1 { Black has no reply against White's multiple threats. } a2 16. Rxa2 $1 { A pretty finish. } Rxa2 17. f7 Ra8 18. Nf6+ { The king is forced onto the back rank. } Kc8 19. f8=Q+ { In this example we saw how the power outpost allowed White to completely neutralise Black's activity along the open file, forcing them into improving their structure. Having established a better position, White kept applying pressure until the opponent eventually cracked. } 
