 { Power outposts are, of course, also useful for minor pieces. This equal position was reached in the game Janowski, D - Capablanca,J (1916). However, White is the side that must be careful. } 21. { -KEY- } exf5 $1 ( { Or } 21. exd5 { , in both cases leading to the same position. } ) 
 ( 21. e5 $2 { In the game Janowski, one of the strongest players of his era, misevaluated the demands of the position and made the mistake that would ultimately cost him the game. } Be7 $17 { Suddenly Black was better, with the bishop pair and the ability to play for pawn breaks on both sides of the board. This game is covered from this position in the 'Endgame Play' chapter towards the end of the course. } ) 
exf5 22. f4 $1 { By exchanging pawns, White opens up the e-file and sets up a power outpost on e5 for his knight. Black also has one on e4, but he cannot reach it. } b5 { This plan proved extremely effective in the game. However, that was because White's move 21.e5? left him without any counterplay. Here, however, White could refute this approach without any real difficulty: } 23. Nf3 Kg8 { Anticipating a check on e5. } 24. Ne5 $11 { White's powerful knight would have given him more than enough resources to combat Black's active positional play revolving around the queenside and kingside pawn breaks of ...b4 or ...g5. By comparison, in the game itself, White's knight was reduced to passivity and played a significant role in Janowski's loss. Now that we have built an appreciation for the value of power outposts, we'll close out the section with two examples of how to fight against them. } 
 0-1

