 { White has a dangerous power outpost on c5. Fortunately, Black has a way to defuse the danger. What move should he play? Author's edit: technically this is not a power outpost, as Black can in theory play the move ...b6 after retreating their queen. In practice however, the move is very challenging to play for Black since not only is their queen blocking this option for now, but also if ...b6 were ever played, then the light squares on the queenside would become terribly exposed. This ties in with the other concept of 'pseudo' outposts, as this is effectively functioning as a power outpost although it is not technically one. } 1... { -KEY- } a5 $1 { Correct! By targeting the b-pawn, Black forces White to play b4-b5, losing the power outpost on c5. } ( 1... a6 $2 { In the game, Black failed to sense the danger and soon landed in big trouble: } 2. Bd3 Be7 3. O-O O-O 4. Nc5 $1 { The power outpost is very unpleasant for Black. Their best bet would have been to go passive with ...Be8, but in the game their desire to eliminate White's powerful knight left them open to a thematic tactical blow: } Bxc5 $2 { The bishop abandons its role as a defensive piece on the kingside. } 5. Bxh7 $1 { The consequences are immediately fatal! Here Black resigned, and while it feels to me like one should test that the opponent can find the right finish, there is no doubt that with best play the position is completely winning for White. For example: } Kxh7 6. Ng5 $1 Kg6 7. Nxf7 Rxf7 8. Qg4 Kh7 9. Qh5 Kg8 10. Qxf7 Kh7 11. Rf3 $18 { With forced mate in two. } ) 
2. b5 { White doesn't have much choice, since the b4 pawn was under fire and bxa5 Nxa5 would have allowed Black's pieces to spring to life on the queenside. } Nd8 $1 { In my view, this is the best square. The knight for now has no squares, but Black intends to go for a pawn break with ...f6 next, after which the knight can reroute to f7. } 3. a4 { Defending the b5 pawn. } f6 $1 4. Bd3 Bb4+ { A precise move, forcing White to retreat a knight or step the king to the centre. } 5. Ke2 Nf7 $11 { In spite of limited space, Black has found great squares for the majority of his pieces and it's unclear how exactly White should manage the pressure against e5. Everything is left to play for here, with Black having fully avoided the positional dangers that awaited had they not destroyed the opponent's power outpost as soon as it appeared on the board. In our final example on power outposts, we'll show a situation where White anticipates the power outpost that may form and takes drastic measures to prevent it. } 
