 { In the following position, Black has a central outpost on d4. For the time being they cannot use it. However, White faces a strategic problem since Black intends to play ...c5 next, converting the d4 square into a power outpost. White senses the danger and takes radical measures to stop the threat. } 1. { -KEY- } f4 $1 { A brilliant move! Recognizing that they cannot stop ...c5 directly, White instead shifts attention to the e5 pawn. } ( 1. a3 $2 { In the game White played this move. However, they soon got into trouble since after: } c5 $1 2. Rd2 Bc6 3. Red1 Nd4 $17 { Black had established a monster knight on the d4 power outpost. This prompted White into playing } 4. Bxd4 { when after } exd4 { Black already had a close to winning position. } ) 
 ( 1. b5 $6 { This move would prevent the power outpost from forming on d4, but is not desirable as now Black would gain a different and very strong outpost on c5. } Ndc5 $17 { Black is significantly better since his pieces are far better coordinated than White's. } ) 
exf4 { Black wins a pawn, so White must have a convincing follow-up to stay in the game. } 2. e5 $1 { A second pawn break, with a double attack against Black's light squared bishop and the f6 pawn. White has succeeded in transforming the position from a largely positional battle to a highly dynamic one. This is often the best approach when you find yourself under positional pressure. } Bxg2 3. exf6 $1 { This in-between move allows White to restore the material balance. } Qf7 4. Kxg2 { A complex and dynamically balanced position has arisen on the board, but one that is objectively equal. Below is a possible continuation: } c5 5. a3 fxg3 6. hxg3 Nd4 { Giving up a central pawn to cut off the support of the dangerous f6 pawn. } 7. Bxd4 cxd4 8. Qxd4 Rxe1 9. Rxe1 Qxf6 10. Qxf6 Nxf6 $11 { The dust settles and an equal endgame is reached. In high-level chess, it's common to see players quickly counteract positional threats with dynamic moves, a strategy that often results in draws. Improving your knowledge of positional chess can transform your perspective of such games. What might initially appear unexciting can become a fascinating struggle as you recognise the positional battles occurring beneath the surface. That wraps up our section on power outposts. Up next, we'll end this chapter by examining another special kind of outpost: the 'pseudo outpost'. } 
 *
